{
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "beat-1",
      "text": "When a rocket falls back to Earth, gravity isn't enough to guide it home safely. Unlike a winged spaceplane, the Falcon 9 first stage cannot glide passively to its target, meaning it must use its engines to steer and slow down."
    },
    {
      "id": "beat-2",
      "text": "Early concepts used parachutes, but extreme atmospheric heating and high-velocity stress destroyed the boosters. Today, its restartable engines burn liquid oxygen and kerosene to actively fight physics."
    },
    {
      "id": "beat-3",
      "text": "Instead of drifting, the booster performs up to three controlled burns. During descent, grid fins steer the aerodynamic lift, while an entry burn slows the vehicle through the upper atmosphere."
    },
    {
      "id": "beat-4",
      "text": "Finally, carbon-fiber landing legs deploy. A final, precise engine firing provides a propulsive cushion, bringing the booster to a soft, controlled landing."
    }
  ],
